WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.709
Hi my name's David Julius, I'm in the Physiology Department

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:13.646
at UC San Francisco. And I'd like to take a few minutes to tell you

00:00:13.647 --> 00:00:17.483
about how we have used spices to understand mechanisms

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:20.319
for temperature and pain sensation. And I thought I'd begin

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.956
just by telling you a little bit about things that have interested me

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:26.759
and what sort of inspired me to work in this area.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.963
So for many years, I've had an interest in natural products

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:35.201
and how these can be used to explore physiology and neuropharmacology.

00:00:35.202 --> 00:00:39.438
And to wonder how people have discovered the use of plants

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:43.309
to folk medicine and other ritualistic behaviors. How the

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.778
active ingredients were identified and how they've been used

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:50.082
in biology to explore signaling pathways. And I just put

00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:53.085
this slide together to show you some of my heroes in this area.

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:57.557
And how they've used natural products from things from opium poppies,

00:00:57.558 --> 00:01:01.727
psilocybin, or marijuana to understand key signaling pathways

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:04.997
in the brain, like opiate receptors, serotonin receptors,

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:10.102
and cannabinoid receptors. And to illustrate the poewr of natural products

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:14.807
for identifying endongenous signaling components in a system.

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:17.043
The other thing that I've been interested in for many years

00:01:17.044 --> 00:01:21.480
like many people in science, is to understand sensory systems.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:25.284
And I think for many of us, including myself, this fascination

00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:29.088
really comes from the idea that sensory systems really determine

00:01:29.089 --> 00:01:32.658
how we perceive our world. The kinds of frequencies of light we can see,

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:36.329
the sounds -- the frequencies of sound we can hear, the

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.833
things that we can taste, are really determined entirely

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:44.237
by the sorts of receptors that we have in our sensory system and how

00:01:44.238 --> 00:01:47.940
that information is integrated in our neural circuits. And

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.710
what's also interesting is that different animals see the world

00:01:50.711 --> 00:01:52.912
in different ways, because they have different receptors.

00:01:52.913 --> 00:01:58.551
A rattlesnake, for example, can see a rodent on a moonless night.

00:01:58.552 --> 00:02:02.588
Because it can detect its body heat, and so I think this raises

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:06.225
fascinating questions about evolution and about how biophysics has

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.161
tuned sensory systems to enable us to live in the environment

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:14.967
that we need to live in. Some years ago, we decided to merge

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:20.406
these interests and to study an aspect of sensory systems that

00:02:20.407 --> 00:02:24.410
at the time was less well understand, than say chemosensor

00:02:24.411 --> 00:02:27.213
systems like olfaction, taste, and certainly vision.

00:02:27.214 --> 00:02:30.516
And that is to understand somatosensation, or sensations of touch,

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:34.720
in particular, to understand this in relationship to how we experience

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:38.424
pain. And the aspect of pain sensation that we focused on

00:02:38.425 --> 00:02:42.728
is nociception, which is the initial act of detecting noxious stimulants. So

00:02:42.729 --> 00:02:47.500
nociception is to pain, for example, what phototransduction is to vision.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:50.503
And we've been interested in this subclass of sensory nerve

00:02:50.504 --> 00:02:53.372
fibers called C and A-delta fibers, the so-called nociceptors

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:56.976
that are the subset of somatosensory neurons that carry

00:02:56.977 --> 00:03:01.013
out the bulk of detection of noxious stimuli that contribute to pain

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:04.784
sensation. And our goal was really to identify the molecules and

00:03:04.785 --> 00:03:07.587
mechanisms that enable these nerve fibers to detect

00:03:07.588 --> 00:03:10.990
things like environmental stimuli, changes in temperature

00:03:10.991 --> 00:03:14.560
or pressure, or noxious chemicals, or even endogenous

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:17.697
things that are produced during inflammation. Endogenous

00:03:17.698 --> 00:03:21.133
chemicals like prostaglandins and other things that generate

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:25.137
or contribute to pain sensation. And the place where this all

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:29.809
comes together for me is really that natural products have played such a

00:03:29.810 --> 00:03:32.812
key role in trying to understand how these nerve fibers work.

00:03:32.813 --> 00:03:38.217
And probably the best example is given by pungent agents from

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:43.623
capsicum peppers, or hot chili peppers. And back in the 70s, Jansco and

00:03:43.624 --> 00:03:47.426
colleagues in Hungary showed that the pungent ingredient in hot chili

00:03:47.427 --> 00:03:52.698
peppers, mainly capsaicin, illicits a sensation of burning pain.

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:56.502
By serving as a specific chemical activator of these nerve fibers that are

00:03:56.503 --> 00:04:00.339
involved in pain reception, mainly the nociceptors. And some years

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:05.444
later, Bevan, Rang, and others in London carried out some biophysical studies

00:04:05.445 --> 00:04:08.514
to suggest that what capsaicin does when it interacts with nerve fibers

00:04:08.515 --> 00:04:11.751
in your mouth or anywhere else in your somatotopic surface,

00:04:11.752 --> 00:04:16.155
is to depolarize the nerve fiber and to allow sodium, calcium ions

00:04:16.156 --> 00:04:19.792
to flow in. And the main questions that we're still unresolved

00:04:19.793 --> 00:04:24.297
by this was, does capsaicin interact with a specific site, a so-called

00:04:24.298 --> 00:04:27.433
vanilloid site? Because capsaicin is a vanilloid compound,

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:32.238
like other pungent agents in peppers. Is there a specific receptor

00:04:32.239 --> 00:04:36.542
for capsaicin? If so, what does it look like? And what might its normal

00:04:36.543 --> 00:04:39.745
physiologic function be in the body? What role does it serve

00:04:39.746 --> 00:04:43.449
in our endogenous ability to sense pain? And we, like many other

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:48.855
people in the field, realized identifying or addressing these questions

00:04:48.856 --> 00:04:51.924
could lead to some key insights into pain sensation, because

00:04:51.925 --> 00:04:55.895
sensitivity to capsaicin was a major functional hallmark

00:04:55.896 --> 00:04:59.765
or characteristic of these nerve fibers that are involved in

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:02.401
making this sensation. In many ways, this was to the nociceptor

00:05:02.402 --> 00:05:07.507
what the T-cell receptor was to lymphocytes. And so we set out

00:05:07.508 --> 00:05:09.709
to do this, but the problem was that nobody really knew what

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.912
this receptor might look like, even if it existed. And so we decided

00:05:12.913 --> 00:05:17.717
to take sort of a structure-blind identity-blind approach to doing

00:05:17.718 --> 00:05:21.554
this. By just exploiting some functional attributes. And the idea

00:05:21.555 --> 00:05:26.492
was that, if the capsaicin receptor does exist, you could make a cDNA library

00:05:26.493 --> 00:05:30.029
from sensory nerve ganglia, and introduce those clones into

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.765
a non-neural cell that's normally capsaicin-insensitive,

00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:37.637
such as a fibroblast. And then if you expose these to capsaicin

00:05:37.638 --> 00:05:41.474
and that allowed calcium to flow into the cell or somehow be

00:05:41.475 --> 00:05:44.243
released from extracellular stores, you could then detect this

00:05:44.244 --> 00:05:48.347
using calcium-sensitive dyes, such as those developed by Roger Tsien and

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:53.419
all those like Indo or Fura. This has inherent risks, the advantage

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.255
is that you make no predispositions about what this receptor,

00:05:56.256 --> 00:05:59.659
mythical receptor, might look like. But the risk is that if the

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.462
receptor is encoded by multiple gene products, the ability

00:06:03.463 --> 00:06:07.533
to transfer a single gene to a fibroblast and recapitulate this activity

00:06:07.534 --> 00:06:10.503
would be very difficult, if not impossible. But you know, you can't do

00:06:10.504 --> 00:06:14.040
science without luck and some risk, and that's kind of what we're all

00:06:14.041 --> 00:06:16.542
in this business for. In some ways, to do something new and exciting.

00:06:16.543 --> 00:06:21.547
And so we tried this and low and behold, we ended up by this method

00:06:21.548 --> 00:06:25.318
of identifying a single cDNA that encoded for a capsaicin receptor,

00:06:25.319 --> 00:06:28.421
which we called the vanilloid receptor. And this is work done by

00:06:28.422 --> 00:06:30.957
a wonderful postdoc in my lab at the time, Mike Caterina,

00:06:30.958 --> 00:06:33.526
who now has his own group at Johns Hopkins University.

00:06:33.527 --> 00:06:37.964
And we could show that this cloned gene encoded a functional

00:06:37.965 --> 00:06:41.100
receptor, because if you introduce it into these cells, and use

00:06:41.101 --> 00:06:44.203
patch clamp electrophysiology to look at what's happening,

00:06:44.204 --> 00:06:46.772
we could show that when you put on capsaicin into these

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:50.109
cells, in fact you induce inward current flow of sodium and calcium

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.146
ions into the cell. And so this told us that the TRPV1 that

00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:57.583
we had identified, this gene, is in fact a vanilloid activated channel.

00:06:57.584 --> 00:07:01.254
But Makoto Tominaga and Mike Caterina and others in my lab then

00:07:01.255 --> 00:07:03.856
addressed really the most interesting question, which is what

00:07:03.857 --> 00:07:07.293
else does this receptor respond to and what might its normal

00:07:07.294 --> 00:07:11.831
physiological role be in our ability to sense noxious stimuli?

00:07:11.832 --> 00:07:15.601
And we tried to activate this receptor with all kinds of things

00:07:15.602 --> 00:07:19.639
that we knew generated pain psychophysically or activated

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:22.909
sensory neurons in culture or nerve fibers. And we went through a whole

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:25.645
gamut of things, neuropeptides, other neurotransmitters.

00:07:25.646 --> 00:07:28.347
And it was only after some time that we realized, gee these

00:07:28.348 --> 00:07:30.983
nerve fibers detect changes in our environment, as well as

00:07:30.984 --> 00:07:34.520
changes internally that relate to pain sensation. And we should try

00:07:34.521 --> 00:07:39.125
some of these things. And the one that we tried that worked was

00:07:39.126 --> 00:07:42.962
heat. And in retrospect, this seems obvious because you describe

00:07:42.963 --> 00:07:47.333
a chili pepper often as a hot chili pepper. And this is a very nice

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:50.570
parsimonious explanation of that. But this wasn't the first thing that

00:07:50.571 --> 00:07:54.040
we thought about, but you know, shortly after failing to look at it

00:07:54.041 --> 00:07:57.176
-- to find results in the other stimuli, it occurred to us that this might

00:07:57.177 --> 00:08:00.746
be the case. And it's really a beautiful result, in that you see that

00:08:00.747 --> 00:08:04.684
the channel, when expressed in this case, in a frog oocyte, is silent

00:08:04.685 --> 00:08:08.187
until you heat the solution, the external solution up, and then the channel

00:08:08.188 --> 00:08:10.923
opens, and you see this large ionic current go through the cell

00:08:10.924 --> 00:08:14.727
with a threshold that's very consistent with the sensitivity

00:08:14.728 --> 00:08:18.531
of heat-sensitive nociceptors to heat. And in fact, is very close

00:08:18.532 --> 00:08:21.734
to the threshold that most of us would describe as the demarcation

00:08:21.735 --> 00:08:24.804
point between determining if something is innocuously warm

00:08:24.805 --> 00:08:28.207
and noxiously hot. So this has led us to propose that TRPV1

00:08:28.208 --> 00:08:30.743
is a little molecular thermometer that sits at the membrane, and

00:08:30.744 --> 00:08:33.713
at least contributes, maybe doesn't underlie all, but contributes

00:08:33.714 --> 00:08:37.583
to our ability to detect ambient temperature in the warm and the hot

00:08:37.584 --> 00:08:40.786
range. Now we all know that we can sense temperatures over a

00:08:40.787 --> 00:08:44.390
wide range, including cold, not just hot. And how might this

00:08:44.391 --> 00:08:46.826
work? We thought that if we could figure this out, we could maybe

00:08:46.827 --> 00:08:49.695
get some larger view of how temperature sensation works.

00:08:49.696 --> 00:08:54.500
And ask if our ability to sense temperatures of different degrees

00:08:54.501 --> 00:08:59.505
is due to a sort of similar, unifying molecular concept or

00:08:59.506 --> 00:09:03.910
represents different molecular mechanisms. And again, we wanted

00:09:03.911 --> 00:09:05.878
to take an approach that was not biased. We didn't want

00:09:05.879 --> 00:09:08.614
to look for channels and genes that were similar to TRPV1,

00:09:08.615 --> 00:09:11.984
but instead, step back and ask how is cold sensed

00:09:11.985 --> 00:09:14.787
from just a functional point of view. And again, the great way

00:09:14.788 --> 00:09:17.023
here, we turned to natural products. Because we all know

00:09:17.024 --> 00:09:21.694
that cooling agents that are derived from mint leaves or eucalyptol,

00:09:21.695 --> 00:09:24.597
produces a sensation of cooling. And Hensel and Zotterman

00:09:24.598 --> 00:09:28.167
in the '50s in Sweden showed that this was the case in neurophysiological

00:09:28.168 --> 00:09:31.404
recordings of sensory nerve fibers. So we played the same game

00:09:31.405 --> 00:09:34.407
that we played with capsaicin to ask if we could identify

00:09:34.408 --> 00:09:38.411
a menthol receptor. And the question was, is there a menthol

00:09:38.412 --> 00:09:43.349
receptor? If so, does it respond to cold? And is it related to TRPV1?

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:45.585
Or is this a totally independent molecular mechanism?

00:09:45.586 --> 00:09:49.655
And so we did, meaning David McKemy, a postdoc in my lab

00:09:49.656 --> 00:09:52.692
at the time, and Werner Neuhausser, ended up identifying

00:09:52.693 --> 00:09:57.163
a clone and a molecule called TRPM8, that when expressed

00:09:57.164 --> 00:10:00.433
in fibroblasts, renders them sensitive to menthol and

00:10:00.434 --> 00:10:03.769
other cooling agents. And as you can tell from the name, TRPM8,

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:06.839
it belongs to the same family of so-called TRP ion channels,

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:12.812
as does TRPV1, it's a molecular cousin. And the question is,

00:10:12.813 --> 00:10:15.715
does it respond to cold? And in fact, again, when you express this

00:10:15.716 --> 00:10:19.018
in a frog oocyte or some other cells, you can see that the channel is

00:10:19.019 --> 00:10:22.488
quiet at warm temperatures, but then opens when you cool the

00:10:22.489 --> 00:10:26.225
cell below about 26 degrees. And so this led to this idea that

00:10:26.226 --> 00:10:30.162
there is some unifying concept of how we sense changes in

00:10:30.163 --> 00:10:35.468
ambient temperature, and TRP channels serve as, or at least contribute

00:10:35.469 --> 00:10:40.339
to our ability to detect changes in ambient temperature over a pretty

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.409
wide range. And they act as little molecular thermometers in

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:48.281
membrane that initiate the polarization of the neural fiber

00:10:48.282 --> 00:10:51.617
in response to changes in temperature. And the best way really to test this,

00:10:51.618 --> 00:10:54.887
of course, is in a living animal with genetics. Using genetics.

00:10:54.888 --> 00:10:57.890
And I think the clearest demonstration of this really comes

00:10:57.891 --> 00:11:00.860
from knock outs of TRPM8, which show a very beautiful phenotype

00:11:00.861 --> 00:11:04.964
in terms of deficits and thermosensitivities. So we made a mouse

00:11:04.965 --> 00:11:08.467
that lacks this channel, this is work done by these wonderful

00:11:08.468 --> 00:11:11.370
people in my lab, Jans Siemens, Diana Bautista, and Sven Jordt, who now

00:11:11.371 --> 00:11:15.174
all have their own labs in Heidelberg, and UC Berkeley, and

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:18.845
Duke University, respectively. And they set up a very nice

00:11:18.846 --> 00:11:22.882
simple paradigm to look and see if deletion of this gene has a

00:11:22.883 --> 00:11:25.785
phenotype associated with it, with temperature sensitivity. So

00:11:25.786 --> 00:11:28.688
here you do this very simple test, where you take in this case, a wild type

00:11:28.689 --> 00:11:32.091
mouse. And you ask if it can discriminate between a warm

00:11:32.092 --> 00:11:35.828
platform and one that's somewhat cooler. Namely 20 and 30 degrees.

00:11:35.829 --> 00:11:39.498
And you can see without any training, this animal avoids the cool

00:11:39.499 --> 00:11:42.869
side and likes to stay on the nice, warm, cozy side. As is true for most

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.839
of us. And he'll stay on this side all day long. Now

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:51.143
if instead, you play this game with a mouse that, in which the gene

00:11:51.144 --> 00:11:54.380
for the menthol receptor is knocked out, TRPM8 deficient mouse,

00:11:54.381 --> 00:11:57.683
you'll see that the animal cannot discriminate between these two

00:11:57.684 --> 00:12:00.286
platforms. And will wander, will spend equal time on each

00:12:00.287 --> 00:12:03.723
of them. In fact, it won't be able to discriminate between platforms

00:12:03.724 --> 00:12:06.626
even when the cool side goes down to very low temperatures,

00:12:06.627 --> 00:12:10.096
to about 10 degrees. Below that, the animal can detect changes,

00:12:10.097 --> 00:12:13.866
can detect the preference for the warm side, which may be due

00:12:13.867 --> 00:12:17.136
to other processes that contribute to cold sensation. But in any case,

00:12:17.137 --> 00:12:20.339
I think this validates the idea that TRP channels, and in particular,

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:23.876
TRPM8, play a really important role as a molecular detector

00:12:23.877 --> 00:12:27.713
of ambient temperature. So through these studies, we've

00:12:27.714 --> 00:12:31.417
been able to identify key elements that enable cells, that

00:12:31.418 --> 00:12:35.054
really enable cells to specify their sensitivity to environmental stimuli.

00:12:35.055 --> 00:12:39.025
And as in other sensory systems, the great things about doing this,

00:12:39.026 --> 00:12:42.762
identifying genes and molecules that specify the sensitivity

00:12:42.763 --> 00:12:48.367
of cells and nerve fibers to stimuli, is that you can use them as

00:12:48.368 --> 00:12:52.138
molecular probes to try and understand the cellular and circuit

00:12:52.139 --> 00:12:56.175
logic, as to how this information is conveyed to the nervous system

00:12:56.176 --> 00:12:58.845
and ultimately, to your brain. So that you can discriminate different

00:12:58.846 --> 00:13:02.715
percepts, in this case, heat from cold. And what these markers,

00:13:02.716 --> 00:13:05.852
antibodies to these channels now tell us, is that the cold and

00:13:05.853 --> 00:13:10.056
hot receptors are really segregated. They're expressed by

00:13:10.057 --> 00:13:15.228
distinct subpopulations of sensory nerve fibers. Which tells us

00:13:15.229 --> 00:13:19.899
at least, at the offset that the information as it comes into the spinal

00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:23.736
chord, at least at the beginning, is segregated. And that information about cold

00:13:23.737 --> 00:13:27.607
and information about heat, is conveyed by two so-called labeled lines.

00:13:27.608 --> 00:13:30.776
Separate lines of sensory nerve fibers. And this at least begins to give

00:13:30.777 --> 00:13:34.847
us some idea as to how behavioral specificity is dictated

00:13:34.848 --> 00:13:41.053
by the circuitry in at least a peripheral aspect of the system.

00:13:41.054 --> 00:13:44.357
So, we've used natural products to identify some important

00:13:44.358 --> 00:13:46.893
players in pain sensation, some of which I haven't had time to

00:13:46.894 --> 00:13:50.296
tell you about today. And all of this work has really come

00:13:50.297 --> 00:13:53.833
about through just sort of curiosity driven research, which by the way

00:13:53.834 --> 00:13:58.504
was funded by you, the taxpayers, via the NIH. And we've just

00:13:58.505 --> 00:14:02.942
been curious about how the system works and allows us to

00:14:02.943 --> 00:14:06.579
appreciate the world around us. But as so often the case, curiosity

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:10.583
driven research can have very practical and important

00:14:10.584 --> 00:14:14.420
therapeutics aspects. And all these channels are now novel

00:14:14.421 --> 00:14:17.156
targets through the development, hopefully, of new types of

00:14:17.157 --> 00:14:20.660
analgesic drugs that we can use to treat a variety of peripheral pain

00:14:20.661 --> 00:14:23.529
syndromes. And that's work that's in progress by a number of

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:28.467
pharmaceutical and academic labs. But hopefully, our goal

00:14:28.468 --> 00:14:31.771
to understand how this system works, and the curiosity-based

00:14:31.772 --> 00:14:34.974
level, will also have some practical outcome that will benefit

00:14:34.975 --> 00:14:37.410
the patients who suffer from common pain syndromes.

00:14:37.411 --> 00:14:40.546
And finally, I'd like to say that, my lab is small. There are maybe

00:14:40.547 --> 00:14:43.416
about -- or relatively small, anywhere between 7 and 9 people

00:14:43.417 --> 00:14:47.653
at any given time. And so the way that we cover the waterfront,

00:14:47.654 --> 00:14:50.489
I'm a big believer in trying to hit a problem from multiple

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:53.726
different angles, and in my lab, we try to do this starting from

00:14:53.727 --> 00:14:57.597
molecular biology through biophysics, through animal behavior

00:14:57.598 --> 00:15:00.666
and anatomy, and physiology, and ultimately structural

00:15:00.667 --> 00:15:03.936
biology. And the way that we do this is to work with other fantastic

00:15:03.937 --> 00:15:07.974
scientists, through close collaborations, some at my own institutions,

00:15:07.975 --> 00:15:12.578
some other places in the world. And this has really allowed us to

00:15:12.579 --> 00:15:15.281
expand our scientific horizons, which is something I think every

00:15:15.282 --> 00:15:18.084
scientist is trying to do. And to work outside the comfort

00:15:18.085 --> 00:15:21.988
zone, and to stretch yourself. And of course, all these people

00:15:21.989 --> 00:15:25.591
are now great friends. And this is a wonderful way to meet people

00:15:25.592 --> 00:15:31.430
and have life-long friends. And also enlarge the sphere of people

00:15:31.431 --> 00:15:35.001
who focus on what your lab can interact with. And so, I'm a big believer

00:15:35.002 --> 00:15:39.272
in collaborative science. And we've had a fantastic time working

00:15:39.273 --> 00:15:43.376
on these projects within my lab and with friends around the world.

00:15:43.377 --> 00:15:44.211
Thank you very much.

